<VI _name="lvdiag_gen_step4b.vi" description="Write direction\2C step 4\3A closes the connection attribute. AssignCtrlToTerm assigns a terminal but leaves its wiring rule at the default\2C which reads back as recommended where the original said required. SetWireRule fixes it - note the getter is GetWiringRule and the setter SetWireRule\2C which is NI's spelling\2C not a typo here. The rule codes were READ off Demo_add with GetWiringRule rather than guessed\3A 1 is required\2C 2 is recommended. A guessed 0 ran without error and changed nothing\2C which is the case where an oracle beats another attempt. Step 3\3A adds front-panel defaults and the connector pane. Values go in with Ctrl Val.Set and are frozen by Default Vals.Make Curr Default - AIXML value= on a Control IS its default\2C so setting the current value alone would not survive. The pane is assigned with {LV.ConnectorPane} AssignCtrlToTerm at the SAME indices the reader found\3A 11\2C 10\2C 3. Step 2\3A a complete\2C runnable VI built by VI Scripting alone. Two numeric controls\2C an Add\2C one indicator\2C wired\2C saved - the Demo_add shape\2C so the proof is that it computes 3 + 4 = 7. Style codes come from docs/vi-object-styles.tsv as plain numbers\2C which works because the style input is a Ring and a Ring is numeric on the wire. A created Numeric is a CONTROL\3A writing {LV.Control} Indicator flips it. Labels are written through {LV.Control} Label -&gt; {LV.Text} Text\2C the same pair the reader uses. Wiring is Connect Wire on {LV.Terminal}. MEASURED\3A {LV.Node} Terminals[] comes back REVERSED against the order AIXML lists them - for Add it is [0]=x+y\2C [1]=y\2C [2]=x. The first attempt indexed 0\2C1\2C2 and produced a VI whose Add output sat on the A control's wire. A production generator must match terminals by Name\2C never by index. Drive it with one string input\3A the full path of the VI to create.">
